window.docContents[204].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Programming___-Proving___-and-Performance--Summary--Provisional-Proofs":{"contents":"Sometimes, it can be useful to pretend that a statement is proved without actually doing the work of proving it.\nThis can be useful when making sure that a proof of a statement would be suitable for some task, such as a rewrite in another proof, determining that an array access is safe, or showing that a recursive call is made on a smaller value than the original argument.\nIt's very frustrating to spend time proving something, only to discover that some other proof would have been more useful.\n\nThe  tactic causes Lean to provisionally accept a statement as if it were a real proof.\nIt can be seen as analogous to a stub method that throws a NotImplementedException in C#.\nAny proof that relies on  includes a warning in Lean.\n\nBe careful!\nThe  tactic can prove any statement, even false statements.\nProving that  can cause an out-of-bounds array access to persist to runtime, unexpectedly crashing a program.\nUsing  is convenient during development, but keeping it in the code is dangerous.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Programming, Proving, and Performance\u0009Summary","header":"8.8.5. Provisional Proofs","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Programming___-Proving___-and-Performance--Summary--Provisional-Proofs"},"/#complete-functor-applicative-monad-exercises":{"contents":"1. Understand the default implementations of , , , and  in  by working through examples such as  and . In other words, substitute their definitions for  and  into the default definitions, and simplify them to recover the versions , , , and  that would be written by hand.2. On paper or in a text file, prove to yourself that the default implementations of  and  satisfy the contracts for  and . In this argument, you're allowed to use the rules from the  contract as well as ordinary expression evaluation.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009The Complete Definitions","header":"5.6.4. Exercises","id":"/#complete-functor-applicative-monad-exercises"}});